  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & >12x \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-12x$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ x(x-12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=12,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/387b02ad3416b06b3f86f12c99b921800ab4b86f39be9da0fc48883952d366d2.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x<0$ or $x>12.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\u22c3(12,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
